{
  "_comment": "Normalized readings of the sibling fixtures, asserted by BOTH the Go suite (cmd/shuttle_remotes_test.go) and the Elixir suite (daemon/test/shuttle/remotes_test.exs). Two readers, one expectation file: a default that drifts in one language fails in both. `port: 0` means absent (Go's zero value; Elixir's nil). `ssh: \"\"` means the entry has NO ssh path at all — ssh defaults to the name only for port-forwarded entries, so a bare `url` remote the operator gave no `ssh` is one the recovery cascade must not try to revive over ssh. `https_proxy` is the document-level `defaults.https_proxy` after parsing, as a {host, port} pair rather than a rendered string: \"localhost:1055\" and \"localhost:01055\" are the same endpoint but different strings, so a string expectation could not say which normalization it meant. {\"host\": \"\", \"port\": 0} means no proxy configured. Host carries no IPv6 brackets. The rejected-input table lives in each suite as a unit test, mirrored line for line, since a fixture file can only hold readings that succeed. `manager` appears only on PORTLESS entries, and only there because it is only there that the two readers must agree: a portless remote has no tunnel to supervise, so both say \"none\" on every platform. For a port entry the two deliberately answer different questions — Go says which supervisor a hub would INSTALL with, the daemon says what its recovery cascade can BOUNCE — so asserting one value would be asserting a bug. `remote_socket` is the remote daemon's unix socket, forwarded in place of remote_port; the two are mutually exclusive, so an entry with a socket reads remote_port 0 (absent) rather than the 4000 default. It is asserted on every row: \"\" means the entry forwards a TCP port.",
